cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "planars: A Python toolkit for deriving morphosyntactic constituency spans from annotated planar structures"
authors:
  - family-names: Good
    given-names: Jeff
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8679-4654"
    affiliation: "University at Buffalo"
  - family-names: Tallman
    given-names: Adam J. R.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3524-9300"
    affiliation: "Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique"
# No version or date-released here: they were hand-kept copies of
# pyproject.toml's version and went stale (0.1.0a19 against 0.1.0a71,
# 2026-11-25). Zenodo's GitHub link records both from the release tag.
url: "https://github.com/jcgood/planars"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/jcgood/planars"
abstract: >
  planars is a Python toolkit for deriving morphosyntactic constituency spans
  from annotated planar structures. It supports cross-linguistic typological
  research by computing strict and loose spans from specialist annotations
  across multiple constituency diagnostics (ciscategorial selection,
  non-interruptability, subspan repetition, stress, and others). The toolkit
  also provides Google Sheets-based annotation workflow tools for research
  coordinators and collaborators.
keywords:
  - linguistics
  - morphosyntax
  - constituency
  - typology
  - planar structure
  - span computation
# The works this one builds on. coding/stage_zenodo.py links every Zenodo
# record of the project to them (as "references") from here.
references:
